$X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$  find the solution of the new system." 13194139536086 true] [17592186166463 1100 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 4; x_{2}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) - 3x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, find the solution of the new system." 13194139536086 false] [17592186166463 1145 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-x_{1}(t) + 4}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{2x_{1}(t) - 3x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-L{x_{1}(t)} + 4L{1}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 2L{x_{1}(t)} - 3L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 1 =-X_{1}(s) + \\frac{4}{s}; sX_{2}(s) - 0 = 2X_{1}(s) - 3X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand we have used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac1s\n$$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for $X_1(s)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsX_{1}(s) - 1 &= -X_{1}(s) + \\frac{4}{s} \\\\\n(s + 1)X_{1}(s) &= 1 + \\frac{4}{s} \\\\\n(s + 1)X_{1}(s) &= \\frac{s + 4}{s} \\\\\nX_{1}(s) &= (s + 4)/(s(s + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for $X_2(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsX_{2}(s) &= 2X_{1}(s) - 3X_{2}(s) \\\\\n(s + 3)X_{2}(s) &= 2X_{1}(s) \\\\\n(s + 3)X_{2}(s) &= 2((s + 4)/(s(s + 1))) \\\\\nX_{2}(s) &= (2(s + 4))/(s(s + 1)(s + 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\dfrac{s+4}{s(s+1)},\\qquad X_2(s) = \\dfrac{2(s+4)}{s(s+1)(s+3)}\n$$" 13194139536086 false] [17592186166463 1145 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{-{x}_{1}(t)+4} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=-L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{1} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=2L{{x}_{1}(t)}-3L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1=-{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-0=2{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\},$ and we have used the fact that $\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac1s.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for $X_1(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =1+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{s+4}{s} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{s+4}{s(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for $X_2(s),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s) & =2{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s+3){X}_{2}(s) & =2{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ (s+3){X}_{2}(s) & =2(\\frac{s+4}{s(s+1)}) \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2(s+4)}{s(s+1)(s+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\dfrac{s+4}{s(s+1)} ,\\qquad \nX_2(s) = \\dfrac{2(s+4)}{s(s+1)(s+3)}.\n$$" 13194139536086 true] [17592186166464 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076259/assets/a699a4e262534557453aaa9e44d1b273dd4489190e50cec8f9ef9e0da91e7d29.png)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\} = -\\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{2}{s-1} - \\dfrac{2}{s}, \\qquad\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{3}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." 13194139536086 true] [17592186166464 1100 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t) - 7; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-2x_{2}(t) + 6$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\} = -\\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{2}{s-1} - \\dfrac{2}{s}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{3}{s}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." 13194139536086 false] [17592186166464 1145 "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{1}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s + 2} + \\frac{2}{s - 1} - \\frac{2}{s}} \\\\\n&=-L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2}} + 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 1}} - 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\\n&=-e^{-2 \\cdot t} + 2e^{1 \\cdot t} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2e^{t} - e^{-2t} - 2 \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{2}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2} + \\frac{3}{s}} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2}} + 3L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\\n&= e^{-2 \\cdot t} + 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= e^{-2t} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2e^{t} - e^{-2t} - 2 \\\\ e^{-2t} + 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536086 false] [17592186166464 1145 "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{